Short:        MSX/MSX2/MSX2+/turbo R emulator, 5.5
Author:       Juan Antonio Gomez Galvez (GUS Entertainment / RED Soft)
Uploader:     z00p (JCEC_1@yahoo.co.uk)
Type:         misc/emu
Version:      5.5
Replaces:     misc/emu/AmiMSX5.lha
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0.0
Requires:     68030 or better, AGA
Distribution: Aminet
Kurz:         MSX-Emulator (MSX2/2+/turbo R), mehr Genauigkeit

AmiMSX 4.x and 5.x are a community update of AmiMSX, the MSX, MSX2 and
MSX2+ emulator by Juan Antonio Gomez Galvez (GUS Entertainment / RED
Soft), released with his permission. It is built from his v3.1 source, with parts
of his later v3.2/v3.5 source. This version puts accuracy first: many
games that broke or glitched in 3.x now run as on a real MSX.

As before, AmiMSX takes over the machine while the MSX runs and drives the
custom chips directly. HELP returns to the menu.

Reporting problems
------------------
AmiMSX runs a large test set well, but some games still have problems
(see Known limits). Please report anything you find as an issue
on GitHub, with the game, the AmiMSX settings and your machine (CPU,
accelerator or emulator), so it can be triaged for the next update:
https://github.com/z00p/AmiMSX/issues

Requirements
------------
- AmigaOS 3.0 or later.
- A 68030 or better. The low-spec target is an A1200 or similar with a
  68030 at 50 MHz and fast RAM, for MSX and MSX2 games: MSX2+ and
  turbo R games are too heavy for the 68030 profile (AGA 030). A stock
  68020 A1200 runs it, but slowly.
- AGA. On ECS AmiMSX warns that it needs AGA and can carry on, with
  limits, as 3.x did.
- The libraries and devices listed in AmiMSX.guide (as for 3.1).
- PiStorm (Emu68), BigMig or another fast CPU for MSX-Music (FM), 60 Hz
  games at full speed and Smooth display.
- For the RTG preset: Picasso96 and a 16 or 32-bit screen mode of at
  least 544x460 (960x540 is best on a 1080p display), and a fast CPU.
- For the MSX turbo R machine (beta, AmiMSX Turbo R): a fast CPU
  (PiStorm, BigMig) and the FS-A1GT firmware, which is not included
  (see below).

Installation
------------
Copy the AmiMSX drawer anywhere and start one of the preset icons:

  AGA 030   68030/50 with fast RAM: PSG, PAL, sprites every frame;
            for MSX and MSX2 games (MSX2+ and turbo R are too heavy)
  AGA       PiStorm, BigMig and other fast CPUs: PSG+FM, display
            frequency from the VDP, Japanese ID (60 Hz), audio
            filter, smooth display
  RTG       the AGA preset on a Picasso96 (RTG) screen, with 50%
            scanlines (ToolTypes RTG and SCANLINES=50)
  Turbo R AGA  the AGA preset with the MSX turbo R machine (beta)
  Turbo R RTG  the RTG preset with the MSX turbo R machine (beta)
               (both start AmiMSX Turbo R)

There are two programs:

  AmiMSX           MSX, MSX2 and MSX2+, for every CPU (the AGA 030,
                   AGA and RTG presets).
  AmiMSX Turbo R   the same emulator with the MSX turbo R machine
                   added (the Turbo R AGA and Turbo R RTG presets).
                   It always starts as the turbo R (the MSX2+ without
                   the firmware), and its own settings file is
                   S:AmiMSXTurboR.prefs, so AmiMSX's S:AmiMSX2.prefs
                   is left alone.

Why two: the turbo R support costs speed on every MSX machine, not just
the turbo R, and on every CPU. AmiMSX Turbo R runs MSX2 and MSX2+ games
1 to 5% slower than AmiMSX (measured on a 68030/50). So AmiMSX leaves
the turbo R out, and AmiMSX Turbo R is for turbo R software.

MSX turbo R (beta, AmiMSX Turbo R only): put the Panasonic FS-A1GT's
firmware in the drawer as Roms/fs-a1gt_firmware.rom (4 MB), and its
kanji font as Roms/fs-a1gt_kanjifont.rom (256 KB, optional; without it
kanji characters are blank). These are the names openMSX uses; the files
tested have SHA-1
  e779c338eb91a7dea3ff75f3fde76b8af22c4a3a (firmware)
  5aff2d9b6efc723bc395b0f96f0adfa83cc54a49 (kanji font).
With the firmware there, the main window's Machine gadget offers
MSXtR. Without it, MSXtR is not offered and a turbo R machine saved in
the settings runs as the MSX2+. PCM sound plays with Sound set to
PSG+FM (as in the AGA and RTG presets).

Each preset keeps its own settings file (ToolType PREFS=<file>), and
Project > Save Settings writes to the preset you started.

Starting a game straight away (from a Shell, a script or a game
launcher such as Lupa):

  AmiMSX [game] [AGA|RTG] [ONEGAME] [MENU] [PROFILE=030|FAST|AUTO]
         [PREFS=<file>] [B=<disk>] [R2=<cartridge>]

  game       the first word that isn't one of these, or a "quoted"
             name (with spaces). A .dsk goes into drive A, a .cas into
             the cassette (Load Tape File's format), anything else into
             Slot 1. It is loaded as the menu would and powered on.
  AGA / RTG  the display for this run, over the RTG ToolType.
  ONEGAME    leaving the game (Help) quits AmiMSX. A game that can't
             be loaded shows the error, then quits.
  MENU       load the game but stay in the menu.
  PROFILE=   030 = the AGA 030 preset's settings, FAST = the AGA
             preset's (RTG's on RTG), AUTO = FAST on a 68040/68060,
             030 otherwise.
  PREFS=     the settings file (also P=); it wins over PROFILE.
  B= / R2=   drive B's disk image / a cartridge for Slot 2.

Before the game starts, AmiMSX waits (up to about a second) until no
key, mouse button or fire button is held.

A game's project icon with AmiMSX (full path) as its default tool
works the same way; its ToolTypes may hold AGA, ONEGAME, MENU and
PROFILE=.

AmiMSX Turbo R takes the same words. In a Shell or a script, put its
name in quotes: "AmiMSX Turbo R" game.dsk PREFS="Turbo R AGA". Use it for
turbo R games; MSX, MSX2 and MSX2+ games run faster in AmiMSX.

Return codes: 0 normal, 5 ran with a fallback (RTG asked for but not
available, the PROFILE's settings file not there, or a turbo R machine
in the settings run as the MSX2+), 10 the game
could not be loaded (with ONEGAME), 20 AmiMSX can't run on this Amiga
(no 68020, no screen or window, a missing library).

Updating from 5.1: copy the new files over the old ones, except the
settings files AGA 030, AGA, RTG, Turbo R AGA and Turbo R RTG if you
want to keep the settings saved in them.

Updating from 5.0: copy the new files over the old ones, except the
settings files AGA 030, AGA and RTG if you want to keep the settings
saved in them, and delete AmiMSX030 and AmiMSX030.info. AmiMSX is now
the program without the turbo R (5.0's AmiMSX030); the turbo R is in
AmiMSX Turbo R, started by the new Turbo R AGA and Turbo R RTG
presets. Copy all the .info files: the AGA 030, AGA and RTG icons
start AmiMSX again, and the icons are new. Shell scripts and launchers
that start AmiMSX030 should start AmiMSX; ones that start a turbo R
game should start "AmiMSX Turbo R".

Updating from 4.9.1: copy the new files over the old ones, except the
settings files AGA 030, AGA and RTG if you want to keep the settings
saved in them.

Updating from 4.8: the preset icons have new names (AmiMSX 030 is now
AGA 030, AmiMSX PiStorm is AGA, AmiMSX RTG is RTG). Delete the three
old ones; settings you saved in them are not carried over.

AmiMSX4.romcache is created next to the program on first use: each
MegaROM is hashed once and its mapper looked up in AmiMSX4.romdb. Leave
the drawer writable.

The included AmiMSX2.data is the original one (MSX, MSX2 and MSX2+ system
ROMs, disk ROM and Kanji), as in the earlier AmiMSX packages, so disk
images and MSX-BASIC work.

What's new
----------
- Z80: POP/RET through ROM, RLD/RRD on ROM, NEG/CPL flags, M1 wait
  states, LD A,R from emulated time.
- VDP: sprite collisions, the 5th sprite flag, sprite mode 2 bands and
  mid-frame splits (sprite tables, SCREEN 4 tables, SCREEN 5/4 modes,
  palettes, R#8/R#18/R#23, display off), transparent copies, LMMC.
- MSX2+: the machine is back, with V9958 fine scroll (R#27) and the left
  mask (R#25) per line (Space Manbow, Nemesis 3 Enhanced, Chikubi Ninja).
- Sound: MSX-Music (FM) on Paula channel 3 ("PSG+FM", or "FM lite" with
  the 4 loudest voices); PSG volume in 3 dB steps as the YM2149.
- Display: double-buffered sprites, Smooth display (SCREEN 4) for fast
  CPUs, automatic edge mask for games that scroll sideways.
- Options: Use Japanese ID (60 Hz), Audio filter, Mask screen edges
  (always or auto), per-game settings next to each cartridge.
- Cartridges: ROM database (mapper by SHA-1), 8 KB, 48/64 KB and 4 MB
  ROMs, flash Konami SCC cartridges with saves (Pampas & Selene),
  ASCII 16K-X flash saves and the FM-PAC's SRAM (Illusion City).
- Disks: drive B with its own image (written back), disk swap with Left
  Amiga.
- Freeware texts and no anti-crack counter, from Juan's v3.2/v3.5.
- Cleaner exits when a screen, window or device can't be opened.

See Docs/CHANGES_4.x.txt for the full list.

New in 5.5
----------
- Input > Joystick as Mouse: the joystick or pad becomes an MSX mouse,
  for games that want one (MSX Legends). The directions move the
  pointer, slowly at first and faster while held; fire 1 and 2 are the
  mouse buttons. It can be saved in a game's own settings.
- Input > Mouse as MSX Mouse: the Amiga mouse in port 1 becomes an MSX
  mouse in MSX port A, and the joystick moves to MSX port B. Both mouse
  buttons work. It takes over from Joystick as Mouse when both are on,
  and it can be saved in a game's own settings.

New in 5.4
----------
- MSX-Music (FM): the drums. The FM chip's rhythm mode (bass drum,
  snare, tom-tom, cymbal, hi-hat) is played, and while the drums have
  channels 7-9 those channels no longer play as instruments (Illusion
  City's harsh sweep every second is gone).
- FM on a 68040 or 68060 with Smooth display on (the AGA, RTG and
  Turbo R presets on a PiStorm): the voices are mixed at twice the
  rate, high notes use tables made for their pitch, and vibrato is
  played, so busy FM music no longer sounds distorted (Illusion City
  with the FM-PAC).
- FM on PAL Amigas: the FM could repeat a short piece every few frames
  (the beam position was misread at line 256; there since 4.0).
- Cartridges up to 16 MB (4 MB before): ASCII 16K-X ROMs over 4 MB
  (Neon Horizon, MSX Legends) and the new NEO-16 mapper (Puzzle Mania XXXL
  and XXXS, 16 MB). Both are found by the signature in their header.
  The whole ROM goes into fast RAM: 8 and 16 MB cartridges are for
  PiStorm, BigMig or a well-stocked accelerator; otherwise AmiMSX says
  "Not enough memory for this ROM".
- Sound Mix Mono and Stereo: loud notes and drums are no longer
  squashed. The volume is set from each frame before it plays, in
  1.5 dB steps; loud music can be up to about 3 dB quieter than in
  5.3, quiet music a little louder.
- Sound Mix now works with Sound chip PSG too (it was PSG+FM only), so
  on fast CPUs, where Mono is the default, PSG games get the software
  PSG with its noise and envelopes. Digitised sound played through the
  PSG's volumes is heard (Puzzle Mania XXXL's music was silent).

New in 5.3
----------
- Game saves: the FM-PAC cartridge with its battery SRAM, in Slot 1
  or Slot 2 (your own FM-PAC ROM, such as AmiMSX 2's FM Pac.ROM). It
  brings FM with it, and its SRAM is kept in FMPAC.PAC in AmiMSX's
  drawer (openMSX's fmpac.pac format). Without one, MSXMUSIC.ROM with
  Sound PSG+FM and Slot 1 empty saves the same way. Tetpuz, Cube Droid
  and Illusion City save to it.
- ASCII 16K-X flash saves: Illusion City's translation saves with
  Load/Save > Flash ROM; the saves go to <cartridge>.flash next to the
  ROM, which is never changed.
- F-1 Spirit 3D Special on AGA: no more grey slab at the horizon (VDP
  LINE ends at the screen side, as on the V9938/V9958).
- VDP status S#2: the TR bit as the V9958 gives it (the old "VDP
  Always Ready" setting is gone).

New in 5.2
----------
- F-1 Spirit 3D Special (MSX2+) plays: the title, the photos, Game
  Disk B after a visit to the menu, the sky in the bends (SP2 with
  R#26) and the player's car.
- MSX2+ YJK modes (SCREEN 10-12), on AGA and RTG, with the nearest of
  SCREEN 8's 256 colours (ZeroNoHoukouR's title, F-1 Spirit 3D
  Special's photos).
- ASCII 16K-X mapper (Slot Mapper menu, ROM database): MSX
  Translations' big ROMs such as Illusion City (up to 4 MB), which
  runs on AmiMSX Turbo R.
- VDP status S#2: HR and VR are read with the V99x8's timing (from
  the right border, 52 cycles, 68 in text modes; VR by the line count),
  measured against openMSX. Snake and Rhino in the Sketchbook now
  loads its own palette (pale colours), as in openMSX.
- Z80: an LDIR that overwrites its own opcode stops there, as on a
  Z80 (Illusion City).
- Sprite bands keep their own pattern table (R#6), and sprite
  colours drawn with VDP commands in SCREEN 5 show in the frame
  they are drawn in.
- The AGA 030 preset is for MSX and MSX2 games; MSX2+ and turbo R
  games are too heavy for a 68030.

New in 5.1
----------
- Two programs, named for what they run: AmiMSX (MSX, MSX2, MSX2+; the
  AGA 030, AGA and RTG presets) and AmiMSX Turbo R (adds the turbo R;
  the new Turbo R AGA and Turbo R RTG presets; it always starts as the
  turbo R). 5.0's AmiMSX030 is now AmiMSX, and the AGA and RTG presets
  no longer pay the turbo R's speed cost.
- Wider preset icons, shaped like MSX cartridges; the turbo R ones say
  "turbo R" on the label.
- Emulation > Speed: Overclock +10%, +25% and +50%, as well as
  Overclock (Max) (the old Overclock) and No Limit. Games that slow
  down on a real MSX (Nemesis 2) lose some of the slowdown without
  running far too fast, as they do at Max.

New in 5.0
----------
- MSX turbo R machine (beta), a Panasonic FS-A1GT with the R800 CPU,
  BASIC 4.1, MSX-DOS 2.31, PCM and the kanji ROM, for fast CPUs, with
  the user's own firmware (see Installation and Known limits).
- Two programs: AmiMSX (with the turbo R, AGA and RTG presets) and
  AmiMSX030 (without it, AGA 030 preset).
- A game on the command line or in a project icon, for scripts and
  launchers such as Lupa, with return codes (see Installation).
- KTRL CD32+ pad type in Input > JoyPort 2 Pad / JoyPort 1 Pad.
- Input > Joystick as Cursor Keys, for games that only read the
  keyboard (fire 1 is Space, fire 2 is M).
- MSX2+ and turbo R: no more flashes in SCREEN 4/5 splits with R#26
  scrolling (S.T.G.), and VDP POINT and SRCH keep their return address
  (Superiority Fighters' VS mode).

New in 4.9.1
------------
- Fixes a crash: SCREEN 2 drawing could clear 256 bytes of the
  emulator's own memory (Nemesis 2 crashed after a few minutes).

New in 4.9
----------
- Z80 fixes: DAA after INC A or DEC A gives the Z80's result (Invasion
  of the Zombie Monsters gave 65 lives instead of the continue
  screen, issue #3), and the R800-only multiply opcodes do nothing on
  the Z80, as on a real MSX.
- Disks: GETDPB returns with carry clear, as the real disk driver.
- The preset icons are now called AGA 030, AGA and RTG.

New in 4.8
----------
- Faster on a 68030/50 (WinUAE's cycle-exact 68030/50, which matches
  a real Blizzard 030/50 within a few points): SCREEN 8 games skip
  picture blocks that haven't changed (Uridium 2 from 77% to 98% of
  full speed), SCREEN 4 games with a table split skip unchanged rows
  below it (Space Manbow from 75% to 79%), and games that wait for
  the next frame in a short loop skip whole passes of it (Lilly's
  Saga from 90% to 98%). PiStorm already ran these at full speed.

New in 4.7
----------
- Quicker response to the joystick and keyboard: RTG frames are drawn
  as soon as they are emulated (3 ms from the input read to the
  drawing on a PiStorm A1200, was about 29), Smooth Display shows its
  frame a frame sooner, and in the other modes on the Amiga output the
  input is read just before the next frame starts (about a field
  sooner on a PiStorm).
- Emulation > Sound Mix for PSG+FM (PSG too from 5.4): Classic as before, or Mono and
  Stereo, which make the PSG in software (noise included) and mix it
  with the FM. For fast CPUs; takes effect at Power ON or Reset.
- FM: R-Type's instruments ring out, and quiet FM music is louder.
- Z80 block instructions (LDIR and the rest) can be interrupted at
  line ends, as on a real Z80 (Space Manbow 2's title split is on
  time).
- The Emulation menu is grouped into speed, sound and display.

New in 4.6
----------
- MSX-Music ROM: put your own 16 KB MSXMUSIC.ROM next to AmiMSX and,
  with Sound on PSG+FM, games find the real ROM and its music driver.
  R-Type then plays its FM music, and Gradius III Legends plays FM from
  disk. Without the file, games that look for MSX-Music still find it.
- PSG+FM only for games with FM: a ROM with no FM in it runs on the
  PSG and keeps the PSG's noise channel. Save Game Settings still wins,
  and disks are not touched. DEVA keeps the Sound you set.
- MSX2+: R-Type runs in Slot 2.
- Sprites: magnified sprites at their real size on the Amiga output
  (Shoulder Blade OVERDRIVE's title, Neon Revenant), and DEVA's hero
  keeps his head.
- Uridium: the whole status bar shows (score, lives and logo).
- Space Manbow 2's title: no dim copy of the logo under it, and no
  frames with the bottom half's picture at the top.
- Zanac EX runs faster on a 68030: in WinUAE's 68030/50 about 94% of
  full speed on average (was 91%), and 81% at its slowest (was 74%).

New in 4.5
----------
- The menus are rearranged into seven: Project, Emulation, Input,
  Disks, Cartridges, Tape and Tools, with plainer names (the guide gives
  the old name of each line, and says more about what the settings do).
  Main window: Machine:, Display: (Auto), Draw: (1 to 5 Frames),
  Sprites ECS / 1 Frame, drives Off / Empty, and Drive A: and Drive B: rows.
  Slot 2 can always be chosen: with an MSX Disk ROM set, it asks to
  switch it off.
- Emulation > Speed: Normal, Overclock, No Limit (was Real MSX, Fast CPU,
  Turbo Mode, in the main window).
- Disks by drive: Drive A (Load, Save, Eject), Drive B (Load, Eject).
  Tools > New Blank Disk asks which drive.
- Gone: the On Quit settings (quitting always puts back the Amiga's own
  display), Save RAM (Slot 3), the Register item, the Imploder compressor
  and other dead windows (packed disk images still load). Settings files
  keep their layout; an old On Quit setting is ignored.

New in 4.5 beta
---------------
- RTG display (beta): the new "AmiMSX RTG" preset shows the MSX on a
  Picasso96 screen, such as a PiStorm's HDMI output: a sharp picture,
  every MSX dot 2x2 pixels (960x540, an exact 2x on a 1080p display).
  The menu opens there too, at double height. SCREEN 0 (40 and 80
  columns, with blink), 1 to 8 are drawn on RTG, so the MSX2 boot
  animation (SCREEN 6) and MSX-BASIC show there too, and so are screens
  split between two of them (Manbow 2's title: SCREEN 8 over SCREEN 5);
  other frames still go to the Amiga's own video output (see Known
  limits).
- Scanlines on the RTG screen, hard or blended. Hard: every second row
  25, 50 or 75% darker. Blended (as in Arnold): the row between two lines
  is the mix of both, 25, 50 or 75% darker (light, normal, strong), which
  looks softer on a big display. The choice is in its own menu item,
  Emulation > RTG Scanlines (Off, Hard 25%, Hard 50%, Hard 75%, Blended
  light, Blended, Blended strong); it takes effect at the next Continue
  or Power ON and Save Settings keeps it. The ToolType SCANLINES=<n> (n%
  darker, hard; the RTG preset has 50) or SCANLINES=blendlight, blend or
  blendstrong is used when the settings file has no choice saved yet.
  Blended scanlines need the 2x picture (a 1x screen gets hard ones) and
  about 230 KB more memory.
- SCREEN 5 on RTG: VDP commands draw straight into the MSX video memory,
  without copying the picture back from the AGA screen each frame.
- R-Type's cartridge type (Irem): R-Type plays, alone or in Slot 2
  behind the R-Type FM loader in Slot 1 (set MSX Disk ROM to None).
  Chosen by the ROM database, or Cartridges > Slot x Mapper > R-Type.
- The menu's About window shows the release date again.
RTG is still new: please report what you find on GitHub (see Reporting
problems), with the preset you used and your RTG board.

New in 4.4
----------
- Per-game machine: an MSX2, MSX2+ or turbo R game without its own
  settings starts on that machine (turbo R games on the MSX2+), from
  the ROM database and a new disk database. File Hunter's MSXdev and
  homebrew games are in both. A game's own settings keep the machine.
- Cartridge SRAM: save memory for the ASCII8/ASCII16 SRAM cartridges
  (Hydlide 2, A-Train, Xanadu, Deep Dungeon...), kept as
  <cartridge>.sram next to the ROM, the same format as openMSX's.
- VDP commands: SRCH (BASIC's PAINT in SCREEN 5 and up); HMMV keeps
  both colours of a byte (Pampas & Selene's health bars); SCREEN 8
  PSET, LINE, YMMM, HMMC and LMMC draw into video memory.
- SCREEN 8 on a 68030/50 is back to its speed before 4.3 (Uridium 2:
  about 74% of MSX speed, 4.3 about 68%).
- 68040/060 (PiStorm, BigMig): SCREEN 2 and 4 tiles are drawn by the
  CPU. Lotus F3's road no longer judders. Bombaman's sprites no
  longer ghost on fast machines.

New in 4.3
----------
- SCREEN 7/8 video memory is stored as on the V9938 (its two banks
  interleaved). Manbow 2's title screen is right: logo, START and its
  arrow, no boxes over the logo.
- VDP commands stay busy as long as on a real MSX2 (they still draw
  at once). Jailbreak: no strip at the bottom of the playfield.
- VDP commands: XOR/AND/NOT drawing modes fixed (Head over Heels
  Remake's menu letters); SCREEN 8 LMMV, DIX/DIY, POINT and LMCM.
- Z80 timing: DAA and DD/FD + LD r,r' cost the right cycles; 957 of
  959 instructions match the Z80+M1 timing table.
- Sprite collisions are checked where sprites are on.
- SCREEN 8 games that switch modes every frame run a little slower on
  a 68030/50 (Uridium 2: about 67% of MSX speed, was about 74%).

New in 4.2
----------
- Gradius III Legends: on POWER METER SELECT the old selection box is
  erased again. VDP LINE now honours the logical operation (XOR, AND,
  OR, NOT and the transparent versions); it always drew solid before.
- Mask screen edges is now Off, 8 dots or 16 dots, per game; Off is
  the default and shows the whole picture. Auto mask is gone (it cut
  off Uridium 2's score panel). The MSX2+ left mask (R#25) is always
  applied.
- Quitting: Screen on Exit 'No Change' puts the Amiga's own PAL/NTSC
  timing back. After a 60 Hz game, PAL screens opened later (other
  emulators, games) lost their bottom lines.
- Options menu: Audio filter and Smooth display no longer run the
  PAL/NTSC screen handlers when picked.

New in 4.1.2
------------
- Uridium 2: the title picture is drawn right. The Z80 now takes a
  pending interrupt straight after the instruction that follows EI,
  as a real Z80 does.
- Sprites: magnified sprites (R#1 MAG) in sprite mode 1 (Bit Logic's
  hero on the intro screen, Popolon).
- SCREEN 2/4: a tile whose pattern or colour changes is redrawn in
  the same frame (Gradius 2 with the MSX2+ enhancement patch: a grid
  of dots over the star field).
- MSX2+: a SCREEN 5 game that doesn't use the coarse scroll (R#26)
  uses the same display layout as on an MSX2.
- MSX2+ coarse scroll: no stray column at the left edge and no jumps
  (Gradius III Legends scrolls smoothly).
- Screen pages: a page switch the game makes in the border is shown
  from the top of the picture. On fast machines Barbarian's fighters
  no longer flicker or vanish.
- Colours: MSX colour levels now use the full Amiga range (white is
  full white), matching an MSX within a step.

New in 4.1.1
------------
- CD32 pads: a menu option per joystick port (Options: JoyPort 2
  (Joystick) Pad, JoyPort 1 (Mouse port) Pad: Standard (2 buttons) or
  CD32 pad). With CD32 pad the port is read with the CD32 protocol:
  red = fire 1, blue = fire 2, yellow = Space, green = M, play = F1.
  No auto-detection. Saved with the prefs and per game.
- MSX2+: sprites in SCREEN 5 are no longer garbled after a VDP
  command touched their tables (Pampas & Selene walking left).

New in 4.1
----------
- MSX2+: coarse scroll (R#26) and two-page scroll (SP2) in SCREEN 5
  and the tile modes (Gradius III Legends: set ROM to MSX2+).
- Mode splits: SCREEN 8 over SCREEN 5 (Manbow 2's title screen).
- Manbow 2: the ship no longer jumps back while it moves.
- Smooth display drops a late frame instead of repeating one, so the
  music keeps its tempo.
- Z80: BIT, IN, RLD/RRD, CPI/CPD, DAA flags; RETI; IM 0; interrupt and
  instruction timings.
- MVD drives are write-enabled by default, so disk games can save.
- A display bug that could blank a whole frame is fixed.

Games it runs well
------------------
Tested on a PiStorm A1200 and in the regression suite (WinUAE):
- Homebrew: Manbow 2, Lilly's Saga, Chikubi Ninja, Delta, Neon
  Revenant, BUN-GA-RUA (Maiden's Extreme Fist), Pampas & Selene,
  Uridium (MSXdev'14), Night Knight, Nogalious, Sword of IANNA,
  Westen House, The Cure, Caos Begins, Uwol, Bit Logic, Mutants from
  the Deep, Shoulder Blade OVERDRIVE, Gradius III Legends (MSX2+).
- Enhanced classics (community patches of Konami games; bring your
  own original): Space Manbow (smooth on MSX2, superb on MSX2+),
  Nemesis 3 Enhanced, Salamander Enhanced, Usas Enhanced, Zanac Ex
  Enhanced, Gradius 2 with the SCC/enhancement patches.
- Disks: Ark-A-Noah and Sex Bomb Bunny (in MVD_Files), disk-based
  homebrew such as Gradius III Legends.
Free homebrew: file-hunter.com and msx.org list most of these.

Known limits
------------
- Smooth display covers SCREEN 4 only; other modes run as before.
- Smooth display switches itself off in games that play MSX-Music
  (FM), so their music keeps its tempo.
- MSX2+: some MSX2 games choose a different video mode on an MSX2+,
  so the picture size differs (Gradius 2, Pampas & Selene); run MSX2
  games as MSX2. YJK pictures (SCREEN 10-12) use the nearest of
  SCREEN 8's 256 colours.
- F-1 Spirit 3D Special on RTG: wrong background colour and streaks
  in the intro pictures (PiStorm A1200; AGA is fine).
- F-1 Spirit 3D Special: the road's top strip at the horizon can
  drift away from the rest of the road on bends (AGA and RTG).
- Nemesis 2: the stars are drawn cyan instead of white.
- Lilly's Saga: on fast machines the save-slot menu text can be drawn
  as solid blocks (seen with Smooth display off).
- CD32 pads: on Standard a pad is read as a plain two-button joystick
  (red = fire 1, blue = fire 2); set the port to CD32 pad for the
  other buttons, and set a switchable adapter to its CD32 mode too.
  A KTRL CD32+ pad (and a pad or adapter that misses or mixes up
  buttons on CD32 pad) wants the KTRL CD32+ setting.
  Left Amiga (port 2) or Right Amiga (port 1) also press MSX fire
  button 2.
- Rarely, a game doesn't start on the first Power ON after the
  cartridge was changed (a black screen): press Power ON again.
- RTG: SCREEN 5 and 7 are drawn on RTG only with "100% Refresh
  in Screen Changes" on (the default); without it those frames go to
  the Amiga's own output, and the RTG screen keeps its last picture and
  goes black after a second. On a screen too small for the 2x picture
  (under 544x460), SCREEN 6, 7 and 80-column text show every other
  dot. 60 Hz games on a 50 Hz display show 5 frames in 6.
- Slot 2 can't be used while an MSX Disk ROM is chosen: AmiMSX offers
  to switch it off.
- Drive B takes plain 360K and 720K images, not .MVD files.
- R-Type's FM music needs the MSX-Music ROM, which isn't included. Put
  your own next to AmiMSX as MSXMUSIC.ROM (16 KB); without it R-Type
  plays with the PSG. The ROM tested, dumped from a running FS-A1WX or
  FS-A1WSX, has SHA-1
  6354ccc5c100b1c558c9395fa8c00784d2e9b0a3.
  The clean image of the same ROM, with FFh in place of the last 16
  bytes, has SHA-1
  aad42ba4289b33d8eed225d42cea930b7fc5c228.
- Uridium 2: one-dot stars can shimmer. The widely shared 2015 build
  has its music switched off by its author, so it plays silent.
- Zanac EX (the original, not the Enhanced version) still slows to
  about 80% in busy scenes on a 68030/50.
- Changing Sound in the menu during a game and pressing Continue
  restarts or crashes the game: change it, then Power ON.
- Puzzle Mania XXXS: the pictures repeat in bands. The game changes
  the text-mode pattern table every 24 lines with line interrupts,
  which AmiMSX doesn't follow in SCREEN 0.
- Cartridges over 4 MB take a while to load on a 68030 (about 20 s
  for 8 MB in WinUAE) and need that much free fast RAM.
- Neon Horizon needs an OPL4 (MoonSound), which AmiMSX doesn't
  emulate: it plays without music, and after a finished race the next
  start can stop on a character picture instead of the title (openMSX
  without a MoonSound does the same). Delete Neon Horizon.rom.flash to
  start it again.
- An MSX mouse in MSX port B (Joystick as Mouse or Mouse as MSX Mouse
  with Swap Joystick Ports on) drifts and doesn't follow the moves.
  Leave Swap off for mouse games.
- Sound Mix Classic (the default on a 68030): digitised sound played
  through the PSG's volumes is silent. Mono plays it.
- MSX-Music (FM): 5.4's cleaner FM needs a 68040 or 68060 (PiStorm)
  with Smooth display on. On a 68030 or a BigMig, or with Smooth
  display off, high notes of bright instruments can still sound harsh
  and vibrato is not played. Tremolo is not played on any machine.

The MSX turbo R machine is beta. Known issues:
- Too slow on a 68030: AmiMSX Turbo R is for PiStorm and BigMig.
- Zone Terra: stays on a blue screen.
- F-Nano 2: black screen.
- The Rise (demo): wrong colours (greens show cyan), the player sprite
  is missing, and the second PSG is not emulated.
- Superiority Fighters: Register mode waits for a microphone (PCM
  recording is not emulated).
- No MIDI, and no speed difference between the R800's ROM and DRAM
  modes.
- Once, a key kept repeating at the MSX-DOS prompt (not reproduced).
- ZeroNoHoukouR: sprites are missing in some frames.
- Illusion City: the borders of the menu boxes have the wrong colours.
- ASO: MSX-DOS 2 does not boot with 512 KB of RAM, and 256 KB is too
  little for the game.
- BASIC: the CALL KANJI screen is blank. Earth (1996): black screen.
- LostWord Episode 0: stays blank on the RTG screen of a PiStorm A1200
  (it runs on the Amiga's own output, and on RTG in WinUAE).


